    {
      "id": "iWjj19WGPp5TJCsZHb6Qy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -586.6394261065079,
      "y": 6105.308034079424,
      "width": 568.899169921875,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "gT6AXYuy0Crh5LFIuWfcS"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5Dl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 433699938,
      "version": 383,
      "versionNonce": 848876386,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790933799506,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "LayerNorm makes the activation distribution much easier\nto quantize reliably. Quantization has only a limited\nnumber of representable levels. Suppose you have the\n“misbehaved” activation:\n\nx = [120, 0.01, -0.2, 0.0]\n\nThe entire quantization range has to accommodate 120.\nThe small values then occupy an extremely tiny portion of\nthat range and may collapse toward the same quantized\nvalue.\nAfter LayerNorm, the values are rescaled relative to the\nstatistics of the vector, so that one extreme coordinate\ndoes not dominate the numerical scale as severely.\n\nx norm = [1.73, -0.57, -0.58, 0.57]",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "LayerNorm makes the activation distribution much easier to quantize reliably. Quantization has only a limited number of representable levels. Suppose you have the “misbehaved” activation:\n\nx = [120, 0.01, -0.2, 0.0]\n\nThe entire quantization range has to accommodate 120. The small values then occupy an extremely tiny portion of that range and may collapse toward the same quantized value.\nAfter LayerNorm, the values are rescaled relative to the statistics of the vector, so that one extreme coordinate does not dominate the numerical scale as severely.\n\nx norm = [1.73, -0.57, -0.58, 0.57]",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hh_EGF2JYsTNEtiiWt0oc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 487.4671074208677,
      "y": 7877.716876643053,
      "width": 594.936279296875,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "EasZ6r-9cEbZIylbkjd2P"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5v4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1652483874,
      "version": 72,
      "versionNonce": 1487673122,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790933799506,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": " Optimized Kernels for 1.58-bit Models",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": " Optimized Kernels for 1.58-bit Models",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "yoeb7-cnSSIAXYwCiy07V",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 203.61363248597172,
      "y": 7947.055107729784,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 1350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "Manzh5FYFPrQN9Mmic90b",
        "wb_n4eS9UdvOSxxj93PTh",
        "EasZ6r-9cEbZIylbkjd2P"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5v8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1548416674,
      "version": 1014,
      "versionNonce": 723575522,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933799506,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Bitpacking is simply a way to store several very-low-bit values inside a normal machine word, such as an 8-bit byte,\ninstead of wasting a whole byte for every tiny value.\n\nFor ternary BitNet weights the theoretical minimum is 1.58 bits/weight\n\nBut CPUs do not have a native “1.58-bit integer” type. So bitnet.cpp uses different packing formats/kernels to\nencode several ternary weights efficiently.\n\n\nI2_S Kernel adopts the vanilla multiply-then-addition manner to perform the matrix multiplication. It transforms\neach full-precision weight into a 2-bit representation offline. During computation, it transforms the weights back to\ntheir original values and performs the vanilla GEMV operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWe need new logic to unpack the bits but we can use old logics for operations\n\nTL1 Kernel preprocesses every two full-precision weights by packing them into 4-bit index, and pre-computes their\ncorresponding activations into 3^2 = 9 values. The index-value pairs are stored in a lookup table to perform LUT\ncomputation. GEMV processing is performed using an int16 LUT and accumulation through addition.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTL2 Kernel is similar to TL1. The major difference is that it compresses every three weights into a 5-bit index,\nwhile TL1 compresses every two weights into a 4-bit index. Therefore, TL2 achieves a higher compression ratio than\nTL1. We recommend using it in environments with limited memory or bandwidth, since it employs LUT and reduces\nmodel size by 1/6 compared to TL1 Kernel, thereby lowering bandwidth requirements.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Bitpacking is simply a way to store several very-low-bit values inside a normal machine word, such as an 8-bit byte, instead of wasting a whole byte for every tiny value.\n\nFor ternary BitNet weights the theoretical minimum is 1.58 bits/weight\n\nBut CPUs do not have a native “1.58-bit integer” type. So bitnet.cpp uses different packing formats/kernels to encode several ternary weights efficiently.\n\n\nI2_S Kernel adopts the vanilla multiply-then-addition manner to perform the matrix multiplication. It transforms each full-precision weight into a 2-bit representation offline. During computation, it transforms the weights back to their original values and performs the vanilla GEMV operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWe need new logic to unpack the bits but we can use old logics for operations\n\nTL1 Kernel preprocesses every two full-precision weights by packing them into 4-bit index, and pre-computes their corresponding activations into 3^2 = 9 values. The index-value pairs are stored in a lookup table to perform LUT computation. GEMV processing is performed using an int16 LUT and accumulation through addition.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTL2 Kernel is similar to TL1. The major difference is that it compresses every three weights into a 5-bit index, while TL1 compresses every two weights into a 4-bit index. Therefore, TL2 achieves a higher compression ratio than TL1. We recommend using it in environments with limited memory or bandwidth, since it employs LUT and reduces model size by 1/6 compared to TL1 Kernel, thereby lowering bandwidth requirements.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
