    },
    {
      "id": "UNXBhSxowhH7bEhcK1YNJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 198.02079202605546,
      "y": 9585.55935435242,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 2525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "uJBAuvswPxUhowlBnw8jS",
        "vgS1eiYrMA3XR2DNs-BgJ",
        "fR4wu21Ah-ax3ndE5wQSN"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1625205602,
      "version": 1387,
      "versionNonce": 1702321378,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933799506,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "BitNet b1.58 2B4T, the first open-source, native 1-bit Large Language Model (LLM) at the 2-billion parameter scale.\nTrained on a corpus of 4 trillion tokens. model weights are released via Hugging Face \n    \n     🤗 BitNet b1.58 2B4T (1.58-bit): bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T: packed weight used for inference only\n     🤗 BitNet b1.58 2B4T (bf16): bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T-bf16: master weight used for training only\n     🤗BitNet b1.58 2B4T (gguf): bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf: GGUF format of used for bitnet.cpp\n     🐱 BitNet b1.58 2B4T Code: bitnet.cpp\n     💬 Demo: aka.ms/bitnet-demo\n\n\nThe core contribution of this work is to demonstrate that a native 1-bit LLM, when trained effectively at scale, can\nachieve performance comparable to leading open-weight, full-precision models of similar size across a wide range of\ntasks.\n\nThe training process for BitNet b1.58 2B4T involved three distinct phases: large-scale pre-training followed by\nsupervised fine-tuning (SFT) and direct preference optimization (DPO).\n\nFor conversational tasks during SFT and inference, the following chat template structure was em-\nployed:\n\n                                    <|begin_of_text|>System: {system_message}<|eot_id|>\n                                    User: {user_message_1}<|eot_id|>\n                                    Assistant: {assistant_message_1}<|eot_id|>\n                                    User: {user_message_2}<|eot_id|>\n                                    Assistant: {assistant_message_2}<|eot_id|>...\n\nThey measure performance on a wide variety of benchmarks classified as follows:\n\n    • Language understanding and reasoning: ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, PIQA,\n        OpenbookQA,and CommonsenseQA\n    • World knowledge: TruthfulQA and MMLU\n    • Reading comprehension: TriviaQA and BoolQ\n    • Math and code: GSM8K, MATH-500 and HumanEval+\n    • Instruction following and conversation: IFEval  and MT-bench\n\nWe compare BitNet b1.58 2B4T with leading open-weight full precision LLMs of similar size. Its nonembedding memory\nfootprint and estimated energy consumption during decoding are substantially lower compared to all the full-precision\nmodels evaluated. It achieves the best results among the compared models on several benchmarks spanning\nreasoning, knowledge, and math capabilities. On other benchmarks, its performance is closely comparable to the top-\nperforming full-precision models\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\ncomparing BitNet b1.58 2B4T against post-training quantized (PTQ) versions of a leading competitor, Qwen2.5\n1.5B, using standard INT4 methods (GPTQ and AWQ). More importantly, this superior memory efficiency does not\ncompromise performance relative to the quantized models. Standard PTQ techniques lead to a noticeable\ndegradation in performance compared to the original full-precision model. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "BitNet b1.58 2B4T, the first open-source, native 1-bit Large Language Model (LLM) at the 2-billion parameter scale. Trained on a corpus of 4 trillion tokens. model weights are released via Hugging Face \n    \n     🤗 BitNet b1.58 2B4T (1.58-bit): bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T: packed weight used for inference only\n     🤗 BitNet b1.58 2B4T (bf16): bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T-bf16: master weight used for training only\n     🤗BitNet b1.58 2B4T (gguf): bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf: GGUF format of used for bitnet.cpp\n     🐱 BitNet b1.58 2B4T Code: bitnet.cpp\n     💬 Demo: aka.ms/bitnet-demo\n\n\nThe core contribution of this work is to demonstrate that a native 1-bit LLM, when trained effectively at scale, can achieve performance comparable to leading open-weight, full-precision models of similar size across a wide range of tasks.\n\nThe training process for BitNet b1.58 2B4T involved three distinct phases: large-scale pre-training followed by supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and direct preference optimization (DPO).\n\nFor conversational tasks during SFT and inference, the following chat template structure was em-\nployed:\n\n                                    <|begin_of_text|>System: {system_message}<|eot_id|>\n                                    User: {user_message_1}<|eot_id|>\n                                    Assistant: {assistant_message_1}<|eot_id|>\n                                    User: {user_message_2}<|eot_id|>\n                                    Assistant: {assistant_message_2}<|eot_id|>...\n\nThey measure performance on a wide variety of benchmarks classified as follows:\n\n    • Language understanding and reasoning: ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, PIQA,\n        OpenbookQA,and CommonsenseQA\n    • World knowledge: TruthfulQA and MMLU\n    • Reading comprehension: TriviaQA and BoolQ\n    • Math and code: GSM8K, MATH-500 and HumanEval+\n    • Instruction following and conversation: IFEval  and MT-bench\n\nWe compare BitNet b1.58 2B4T with leading open-weight full precision LLMs of similar size. Its nonembedding memory footprint and estimated energy consumption during decoding are substantially lower compared to all the full-precision models evaluated. It achieves the best results among the compared models on several benchmarks spanning reasoning, knowledge, and math capabilities. On other benchmarks, its performance is closely comparable to the top-performing full-precision models\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\ncomparing BitNet b1.58 2B4T against post-training quantized (PTQ) versions of a leading competitor, Qwen2.5 1.5B, using standard INT4 methods (GPTQ and AWQ). More importantly, this superior memory efficiency does not compromise performance relative to the quantized models. Standard PTQ techniques lead to a noticeable degradation in performance compared to the original full-precision model. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
